data.content:
This is a **C# 14 span overload resolution** breaking change.

## What happened

In C# 14, new implicit span conversions mean `array.Contains(num)` now resolves to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

`MemoryExtensions.Contains` **cannot be represented in a LINQ expression tree**, so the expression tree compilation throws at runtime (`NotSupportedException`).

## How to fix

Force the call to bind to `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Use AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Cast to IEnumerable<T>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 3: Call Enumerable.Contains explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these prevents the compiler from picking the span-based overload, keeping the expression tree valid.
